<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/18th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Sainte-Foy</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1760">1760</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of Sainte-Foy was fought on 28 April 1760 just outside <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> City, when French forces under the Chevalier de Lévis defeated the British garrison under James Murray. It was the last French victory of the war in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a>, but Lévis could not retake Quebec once British ships arrived in May, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> surrendered at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a> in September.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-sainte-foy.jpg" alt="Battle of Sainte-Foy" width="320" height="221" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: George B. Campion (1796 - 1870) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Battle_of_Sainte-Foy.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-france">New France</a> had suffered heavy setbacks in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seven-years-war">Seven Years' War</a>. Louisbourg fell to a siege in 1758 and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-duquesne">Fort Duquesne</a> was abandoned; in 1759 Fort Carillon and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fort-niagara">Fort Niagara</a> were taken, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> fell after a long siege and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-the-plains-of-abraham">Battle of the Plains of Abraham</a> on 13 September. The French army regrouped at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>, while the British garrison left in Quebec after the fleet sailed suffered from hunger and scurvy in a city it had largely destroyed.</p><p>In April 1760 Lévis marched back on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> with regulars and Canadien militia, hoping to besiege the town until a French fleet arrived. Murray judged his army too small to hold the walls, which had not been improved since the capture, and took 3,886 men and more than twenty <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> out to the position Montcalm had held in 1759, choosing to attack rather than wait.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>About 5,000 French soldiers with only three <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cannon">cannon</a> were on the field. Seeing them still deploying, Murray advanced. His light infantry drove French grenadiers out of a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/windmill">windmill</a> on the right but were scattered by the French left, and a bitter fight for the windmill went on. On the left the British took some redoubts before Lévis counterattacked with his right wing. Murray sent in his last reserves, but his left gave way after heavy losses and the line collapsed from left to right.</p><p>The British guns held the French back at first, but they began to run out of ammunition, and the ammunition carts were bogged in the snow and mud. The British spiked and abandoned their guns as they retreated. They lost 292 killed and the French 193; three quarters of the officers of the Fraser Highlanders were killed or wounded.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The British withstood a weak siege behind the walls until naval reinforcements arrived in May. No French fleet came: French naval hopes had been ended at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-quiberon-bay">Quiberon Bay</a>, and the few supply ships were lost in the Battle of Restigouche. Lévis raised the siege and fell back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/montreal">Montreal</a>. In 1863 the Monument des Braves in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> commemorated the battle, the start of a wave of commemorations across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> meant to bring Anglophones and Francophones closer together.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Sainte-Foy” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
